{%- doc -%}
  The bordered card the product page uses three times (Figma: Standart Product
  Page → Gift Message, Estimated Shipping, Ingredients & Allergens, Storage).

  One shape, three jobs: a titled white card with a hairline light-blue edge, an
  optional glyph beside the title, and an optional disclosure. The file draws the
  four cards with the same chrome and different insides, so they are one component
  with parameters rather than four snippets that would drift apart.

  Content arrives as captured markup rather than as a slot, since Liquid has none:

  ```liquid
  {%- capture panel_body -%}...{%- endcapture -%}
  {% render 'ui-panel', title: 'Storage', icon: 'thermometer', body: panel_body %}
  ```

  `intro` is the part that stays visible when the card is closed, and `body` is
  what opening it reveals. Both arrive as markup and both get their own wrapper, so
  a caller can hand over several elements and have them spaced — a `<details>` set
  to `display: flex` cannot space them itself, because Chrome collects everything
  after the `<summary>` into one `::details-content` box. With `collapsible` the two are a real `<details>`, so
  the open state, the keyboard and Escape come from the browser — the same bargain
  the filter and variant disclosures take, and still no new script.

  The whole head is the click target, not just the caret. The file draws a caret
  at the far end and a line of copy beneath the title, and a card whose copy is
  inert while the 14px caret beside it toggles is a smaller target than it looks.

  `variant` is the skin. `card` is the product page's bordered white card; `bar` is
  the filled light-blue row the FAQ stacks (Figma: FAQ → Questions). They are one
  component because everything hard about a disclosure is shared — the `<summary>`
  reset, the two `base.css` rules that outrank a lone class, and the
  `::details-content` animation — and what differs is a background, a padding, a
  type scale and which glyph marks the toggle. A second snippet would have copied
  the first list to change the second.

  `animated` moves the card open and shut rather than snapping, using the same
  `::details-content` recipe and the same timing tokens `base.css` already gives
  Horizon's own accordions — so a card here and an accordion elsewhere on the site
  move at one speed, and it still costs no script. It is a parameter rather than
  the default because not every disclosure wearing this card is an accordion: the
  gift message card reveals a field someone is about to type into, and sliding it
  into place under them is worse than putting it there.

  @param {string} title - The card's heading.
  @param {string} [variant] - `card` for the bordered white card, `bar` for the filled FAQ row. Defaults to `card`.
  @param {string} [icon] - Icon asset name, with or without the `icon-ui-` prefix and extension.
  @param {string} [intro] - Markup shown whether the card is open or closed.
  @param {string} [body] - Markup shown below `intro`; hidden until opened when `collapsible`.
  @param {boolean} [collapsible] - Renders the card as a `<details>` disclosure.
  @param {boolean} [animated] - Opens and closes over time instead of at once.
  @param {boolean} [open] - Starts a collapsible card open.
  @param {boolean} [chevron] - Draws the toggle mark. Defaults to `collapsible`. Which mark is drawn is the variant's: a caret on `card`, a plus that becomes a minus on `bar`.
  @param {string} [padding] - `default` for the drawn 14px, `tight` for the 12/16 the shipping card takes.
  @param {string} [id] - The `id` attribute.
  @param {string} [class] - Additional CSS classes.
  @param {string} [attributes] - Extra HTML attributes to render on the element.

  @example
  {% render 'ui-panel', title: 'Estimated shipping', icon: 'shipping-fast', padding: 'tight', body: rows %}
  {% render 'ui-panel', title: 'Storage', icon: 'thermometer', collapsible: true, intro: lede, body: full %}
  {% render 'ui-panel', variant: 'bar', title: 'Can I customize my order?', collapsible: true, animated: true, body: answer %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign panel_icon = icon
  if panel_icon != blank
    unless panel_icon contains 'icon-ui-'
      assign panel_icon = 'icon-ui-' | append: panel_icon
    endunless
    unless panel_icon contains '.svg'
      assign panel_icon = panel_icon | append: '.svg'
    endunless
  endif

  assign show_chevron = chevron
  if chevron == nil
    assign show_chevron = collapsible
  endif

  assign panel_variant = variant | default: 'card'
  assign panel_classes = 'ui-panel ui-panel--' | append: panel_variant

  # The bar's padding is the variant's own. Writing the padding modifier as well
  # would leave two single-class rules setting one property, and which of them won
  # would be source order inside the bundled stylesheet — a thing this kit has been
  # bitten by before. So the class it would fight with is simply not written.
  unless panel_variant == 'bar'
    assign panel_padding = padding | default: 'default'
    assign panel_classes = panel_classes | append: ' ui-panel--pad-' | append: panel_padding
  endunless
  if collapsible and animated
    assign panel_classes = panel_classes | append: ' ui-panel--animated'
  endif
  if class != blank
    assign panel_classes = panel_classes | append: ' ' | append: class
  endif
-%}

{%- capture panel_head -%}
  <div class="ui-panel__title-row">
    <span class="ui-panel__title">
      {%- if panel_icon != blank -%}
        <span class="ui-panel__icon" aria-hidden="true">{{- panel_icon | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {{ title }}
    </span>
    {%- if show_chevron -%}
      {%- if panel_variant == 'bar' -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Both glyphs are in the markup and CSS shows one of them. Swapping a single
          icon on `[open]` would take either a script or a second request; a pair of
          inline SVGs takes neither, and the kit already carries both — the file's
          plus is the same glyph the quantity stepper uses, drawn larger.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="ui-panel__marker" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="ui-panel__marker-glyph ui-panel__marker-glyph--shut">
            {{- 'icon-ui-plus.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
          </span>
          <span class="ui-panel__marker-glyph ui-panel__marker-glyph--open">
            {{- 'icon-ui-minus.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
          </span>
        </span>
      {%- else -%}
        <span class="ui-panel__chevron" aria-hidden="true">{{- 'icon-ui-chevron-down.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
  {%- if intro != blank -%}{{ intro }}{%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- if collapsible -%}
  <details
    class="{{ panel_classes }}"
    {%- if id != blank %} id="{{ id }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if open %} open{% endif -%}
    {%- if attributes != blank %} {{ attributes }}{% endif -%}
  >
    <summary class="ui-panel__head">{{ panel_head }}</summary>
    {%- if body != blank -%}<div class="ui-panel__body">{{ body }}</div>{%- endif -%}
  </details>
{%- else -%}
  <div
    class="{{ panel_classes }}"
    {%- if id != blank %} id="{{ id }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if attributes != blank %} {{ attributes }}{% endif -%}
  >
    <div class="ui-panel__head">{{ panel_head }}</div>
    {%- if body != blank -%}<div class="ui-panel__body">{{ body }}</div>{%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  :root {
    --ui-panel-gap: 10px;
    --ui-panel-icon: 16px;
    --ui-panel-title-size: 12px;
  }

  /*
   * What a variant is allowed to move sits on the block as custom properties, so a
   * variant redeclares values rather than repeating rules. Everything below this
   * — the summary reset, the two base.css fixes, the animation — is the same for
   * both skins, which is why they are one component.
   */
  .ui-panel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--ui-panel-gap);
    box-sizing: border-box;
    padding: var(--ui-panel-padding, 14px);
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    /* The card is the one place in the kit drawn with a full 1px edge rather than
       the 0.5px hairline, and in the palette's light blue rather than the border
       gray. The bar has no edge at all — it is a fill. */
    box-shadow: var(--ui-panel-edge, inset 0 0 0 1px var(--ui-color-light-blue));
    background-color: var(--ui-panel-background, var(--ui-color-white));
  }

  .ui-panel--pad-default {
    --ui-panel-padding: 14px;
  }

  .ui-panel--pad-tight {
    --ui-panel-padding: 12px 16px;
  }

  /*
   * The filled row the FAQ stacks. Padding is the variant's own and the `padding`
   * parameter does not reach it — the two are not the same decision, and a bar at
   * the card's 14px is not a bar.
   */
  .ui-panel--bar {
    --ui-panel-gap: 16px;
    --ui-panel-padding: 24px;
    --ui-panel-background: var(--ui-color-light-blue);
    --ui-panel-edge: none;
    --ui-panel-title-font: var(--ui-font-emphasis);
    --ui-panel-title-weight: var(--ui-font-emphasis-weight);
    --ui-panel-title-size: 20px;
    --ui-panel-title-case: none;
    --ui-panel-title-line-height: var(--ui-line-height);
  }

  /*
   * The file draws the bar only at 1440. Held at the drawn 20px question inside 24px
   * of padding, a phone spends 48 of its 343 on air and the longest question runs to
   * three lines — so the bar steps down at the theme's own 750, the way every other
   * piece of type in the kit does. The mark keeps its 24px: it is the tap target.
   */
  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .ui-panel--bar {
      --ui-panel-padding: 20px;
      --ui-panel-title-size: 18px;
    }
  }

  /* The bar's inside is copy by definition, so the type belongs to the component
     rather than to whoever hands over the markup. */
  .ui-panel--bar .ui-panel__body {
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-size: var(--ui-panel-body-size, 16px);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    line-height: var(--ui-line-height);
  }

  .ui-panel--bar .ui-panel__body > * {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ui-panel--bar .ui-panel__body a {
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  /*
   * `align-items` and `padding-block` are here because base.css styles the bare
   * element: `summary { display: flex; align-items: center; padding-block:
   * var(--padding-sm) }`. A class outranks that, but only for the properties it
   * actually names — left unsaid, the centring stayed and shrank every row in the
   * head to its own width, which read as a heading mysteriously set in the middle
   * of the card.
   */
  .ui-panel__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: var(--ui-panel-gap);
    padding-block: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .ui-panel__head::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  details.ui-panel > .ui-panel__head {
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /*
   * The body is wrapped and carries the spacing itself rather than leaning on the
   * panel's own `gap`. Chrome puts everything after a `<summary>` inside a
   * `::details-content` box, so a flex `<details>` has two items — the summary and
   * that box — no matter how many elements are written between them. The gap
   * landed above the box and nothing inside it, which left the rule sitting flush
   * against the copy it is supposed to be 10px clear of.
   */
  .ui-panel__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--ui-panel-gap);
  }

  /*
   * Opening and closing over time. The recipe is Horizon's, from the
   * `accordion-custom` block of base.css — `::details-content` sized from 0 to
   * `auto` with `interpolate-size`, `content-visibility` flipped discretely at the
   * end so the copy is still there on the way out, and `@starting-style` to give
   * the opening direction somewhere to start from. Same tokens too, so these cards
   * and the theme's own accordions move at one speed.
   *
   * A browser without `::details-content` never matches these rules at all and gets
   * today's instant open, which is the right way round for a progressive nicety.
   */
  .ui-panel--animated::details-content {
    block-size: 0;
    padding-block-start: 0;
    overflow-y: clip;
    opacity: 0;
    interpolate-size: allow-keywords;
    transition: content-visibility var(--animation-speed-slow) allow-discrete,
      padding-block-start var(--animation-speed-slow) var(--animation-easing),
      opacity var(--animation-speed-slow) var(--animation-easing),
      block-size var(--animation-speed-slow) var(--animation-easing);
  }

  .ui-panel--animated[open]::details-content {
    block-size: auto;
    padding-block-start: var(--ui-panel-gap);
    opacity: 1;

    @starting-style {
      block-size: 0;
      padding-block-start: 0;
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /*
   * Not a flex container, unlike the rest of the panels. As a flex item the content
   * box is sized by flex layout, which quietly wins over the `block-size` being
   * animated — the copy faded in and the padding grew, but the card itself snapped
   * to full height. As a block the animated property is the one that decides.
   *
   * Nothing is lost by it: the space above the revealed part is padding on the
   * content box rather than the panel's row gap, since a closed `<details>` still
   * lays out a zero-height content box and a gap there would leave 10px of dead air
   * under every shut card. Both the head and the body run their own flex inside.
   */
  details.ui-panel--animated {
    display: block;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .ui-panel--animated::details-content {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .ui-panel__title-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .ui-panel__title {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    color: var(--ui-panel-title-color, var(--ui-color-primary));
    font-family: var(--ui-panel-title-font, var(--ui-font-accent));
    font-size: var(--ui-panel-title-size);
    font-weight: var(--ui-panel-title-weight, 600);
    line-height: var(--ui-panel-title-line-height, 1.35);
    text-transform: var(--ui-panel-title-case, uppercase);
  }

  .ui-panel__icon {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: var(--ui-panel-icon);
    height: var(--ui-panel-icon);
  }

  .ui-panel__icon > svg,
  .ui-panel__chevron > svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .ui-panel__chevron {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14px;
    height: 8px;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
    transition: rotate var(--animation-values);
  }

  .ui-panel[open] > .ui-panel__head .ui-panel__chevron {
    rotate: 180deg;
  }

  /*
   * The bar's toggle. Both glyphs are rendered and one is shown, so the swap costs
   * no script — and because the pair is the same drawing minus a stroke, the
   * horizontal bar does not move between the two states.
   */
  .ui-panel__marker {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    inline-size: var(--ui-panel-marker, 24px);
    block-size: var(--ui-panel-marker, 24px);
    color: var(--ui-panel-marker-color, var(--ui-color-primary));
  }

  .ui-panel__marker-glyph {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
  }

  .ui-panel__marker-glyph > svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .ui-panel__marker-glyph--open,
  .ui-panel[open] > .ui-panel__head .ui-panel__marker-glyph--shut {
    display: none;
  }

  .ui-panel[open] > .ui-panel__head .ui-panel__marker-glyph--open {
    display: block;
  }

  /*
   * base.css carries `summary:hover { color: var(--color-primary-hover) }` at
   * (0,1,1), which outranks a lone class and would repaint the title through a
   * hover. Naming the element matches that weight and wins on source order.
   */
  summary.ui-panel__head:hover {
    color: inherit;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
